{"nodes": [{"id": "supabase_functions_fn_confirm_transport_receipt_fn_confirm_transport_receipt", "label": "fn_confirm_transport_receipt", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "supabase/functions/fn_confirm_transport_receipt.sql", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Card ^ref-22 / D06, UAT-11, BR12, R22: the receiving side signs for what actually arrived. A partial receipt is not an error \u2014 the shortfall stays honestly in IN_TRANSIT and partial_receipt_allowed (per-location config) decides whether it may be recorded at all. The TRANSFER_IN posts the RECEIVED weight, never the dispatched one, or the function would conjure the shortfall into the destination's stock; the one exception is an over-delivery, where least() clears only what went onto the truck (drawing more is refused by fn_post_ledger's non-negative guard, R3/BR24) and the surplus is posted as an ADJUSTMENT carrying the receiver's reason, because calling it TRANSFER_IN would say meat travelled on a truck that never carried it. Mode is ALERT, never BLOCK: a receipt is a fact that already happened and refusing it does not make the meat reappear. The verdict comes from fn_check_variance and never from transport_lines.variance_pct, which rounds to 4 decimals and compares raw \u2014 they disagree at 20.004% and one line cannot have two verdicts (TC-21). p_event_date is a parameter because the run's event_date is the DISPATCH date and both config lookups plus the ledger business_date need the receipt's own date (R12). Role comes from the DESTINATION location kind, not from an argument. The receipt carries its own second idempotency key under `select ... for update`, which is what makes two concurrent receipts resolve to one winner and one named refusal rather than two TRANSFER_IN rows (TC-41). Arriving stock lands FROZEN, not READY, because R14 moves weight FROZEN -> READY at the branch."}], "edges": [{"source": "supabase_functi